province = 53
blurb = "Depicts a steep road in the Sayo Pass in Nissaka (present-day Kakegawa, Shizuoka prefecture), a station on the Tōkaidō. At the foot of the trail, people are examining a large boulder.

This boulder was a well-known landmark called the \"night-crying stone\". It is named after the legend of a pregnant woman who was murdered by bandits and spilled her blood on the stone. In one interpretation of the legend it is said that her spirit was contained in the stone and she wept each night. In another version the stone cries every night for the murdered woman.

The famous poet Bashō wrote a haiku about the Sayo pass:

[i]Asleep on horseback
dreams linger, moon distant
smoke [from a fire] for tea[/i]

The poem is an allusion to a verse by the Chinese Tang dynasty poet Du Mu called Early Departure:

[i]I keep my whip hanging and trust the way my horse walks.
For several li, no rooster is heard.
We move through a grove, still half-dreaming,
when the leaves suddenly fall on me.[/i]"
metadata/_custom_type_script = "uid://6kpsqyb2pk7a"
